801: 3.4 Guidance on theatrical skills
802: 
803: 3.4.1 Performer
804: 
805: Students who have chosen performing as a specialism are expected to:
806: 
807: •
808: 
809: learn how to commit dialogue to memory for devised performances and/or learn text they are
810: performing for text-based performances
811: 
812: • develop the ability to interpret and/or create and perform a character as appropriate to the
813: 
814: demands of the performance
815: 
816: • develop a range of vocal skills and techniques eg clarity of diction, inflection, accent,
817: 
818: intonation and phrasing; pace, pause and timing; projection, pitch; emotional range; song
819: and/or choral speaking
820: 
821: • develop a range of physical skills and techniques eg movement, body language, posture,
822: 
823: gesture, gait, co-ordination, stillness, timing, control; facial expression; eye contact, listening,
824: expression of mood; spatial awareness; interaction with other performers; dance and choral
825: movement
826: 
827: • develop an appropriate performer/audience relationship and ensure sustained engagement
828: 
829: throughout the performance
830: 
831: • adopt the latest safe working practices.
832: 
833: 3.4.2 Lighting designer
834: 
835: Students who have chosen lighting design as a specialism are expected to:
836: 
837: •
838: 
839: learn how to design and realise lighting that contributes positively to the overall effect of the
840: performance and communicates intended meaning for an audience
841: 
842: • develop the ability to design and realise lighting capable of establishing the location, time
843: 
844: and/or to enhance mood or atmosphere
845: 
846: • develop the ability to design and realise a range of lighting effects eg through the use of
847: 
848: colour, gobos, gels, filters, gauzes, projections and lighting states through intensity, fading
849: and cross-fading, blackout, shadow, directional lighting
850: 
851: • develop the ability to select the appropriate equipment and determine its position in order to
852: realise the intended design eg choice of lanterns and lamps – profile, Fresnel, flood, moving
853: light, birdies, strobes; rigged, floor and side lights; angle and focus of lanterns
854: 
855: • develop an understanding of how to apply rigging, positioning, angling and focusing
856: • adopt the latest safe working practices.
857: 
858: It is the student's lighting design that is assessed. Although students are expected to operate the
859: lighting equipment when possible, this will not form part of the assessment.
860: 
861: 3.4.3 Sound designer
862: 
863: Students who have chosen sound design as a specialism are expected to:
864: 
865: •
866: 
867: learn how to design and realise sounds that contribute positively to the overall effect of the
868: performance and communicate intended meaning for an audience
869: 
870: • develop the ability to design and realise sounds capable of establishing the location, period,
871: 
872: time and/or to enhance the mood or atmosphere
873: 
874: • develop the ability to select the appropriate equipment to realise the intended sound design
875: 
876: eg use of microphones, amplifiers, software, musical or percussion instruments
877: 
878: Visit for the most up-to-date specification, resources, support and administration   17
879: 
880: • develop the ability to deploy different types of sounds as appropriate to realise the intended
881: 
882: sound design eg live and recorded sound, directional sound, music
883: 
884: • develop the ability to deploy a range of sound effects to realise the intended sound design
885: through eg audio effects, edited and/or mixed sound, distortion, reverb, echo, volume/
886: amplification, fades, soundscapes
887: 
888: • adopt the latest safe working practices.
889: 
890: It is the student's sound design that is assessed. Although students are expected to operate the
891: sound equipment when possible, this will not form part of the assessment.
892: 
893: 3.4.4 Set designer
894: 
895: Students who have chosen set design as a specialism are expected to:
896: 
897: •
898: 
899: learn how to design and realise sets that contribute positively to the overall effect of the
900: performance and communicate intended meaning for an audience
901: 
902: • develop the ability to design and realise sets capable of establishing the location, period, time
903: 
904: and/or to enhance the mood or atmosphere
905: 
906: • develop the ability to select the appropriate materials and equipment to realise the set design
907: 
908: eg flats, cyclorama, floor coverings, drapes, furnishings, projections/media
909: 
910: • develop the ability to realise sets showing an understanding of stage configuration,
911: 
912: performer/audience relationship, action taking place, positioning of entrances/exits, effective,
913: smooth and fluent scene changes, use of space, scale, levels
914: 
915: • develop the ability to design and realise sets selecting as appropriate dressings, furnishings,
916: 
917: material, colour, texture, props
918: 
919: • adopt the latest safe working practices.
920: 
921: It is the student's set design that is assessed. Although students are expected to construct the set
922: when possible, this will not form part of the assessment.
923: 
924: 3.4.5 Costume designer
925: 
926: Costume designs may include masks, hair and make-up as well as clothing.
927: 
928: Students who have chosen costume design as a specialism are expected to:
929: 
930: •
931: 
932: learn how to design and realise costumes that contribute positively to the overall effect of the
933: performance and communicate intended meaning for an audience
934: 
935: • develop the ability to design and realise costumes capable of establishing the character,
936: 
937: period, location and/or contributing to the mood or atmosphere as appropriate
938: • develop the ability to select appropriate materials to realise costumes showing an
939: 
940: understanding of fabrics, textures, trimmings, accessories etc
941: 
942: • develop the ability to create costumes showing an understanding of colour, shape,
943: 
944: appropriate fit, period detail, ethnic authenticity, movement constraints
945: 
946: • develop the ability to create costumes showing an understanding of the intentions of the
947: 
948: performance, individual performer requirements, theatrical devices
949: 
950: • adopt the latest safe working practices.
951: 
952: Where students include make-up and hair in their designs they are expected to:
953: 
954: • develop the ability to select appropriate materials to realise their make-up design showing an
955: understanding of different types of make-up eg grease-paint, liquids, powders, facial hair,
956: eyelashes, gum, putty, prosthetics, stage blood
957: 
958: • develop the ability to create character through make-up: aging, fantasy characters
959: • develop the ability to create hairstyles appropriate to character, period, age, ethnicity.
960: 
